(7279611, 1133179, 30364, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by bmartuch:</b></div><hr/><i> This is very cool. I think it would be better without the vignetting.</i></td></tr></table> <br/><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by posthumous:</b></div><hr/><i> vignette seems too heavy to me but I love the composition. </i></td></tr></table> <br/><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by insteps:</b></div><hr/><i> I don''t necessarily dislike the heavy vignette but I''d be interested in seeing the natural image. </i></td></tr></table> <br/>I don''t know why I like severe vignettes so much; they''re just an affectation when done to this extent, and I know it.<br/>But I actually like the brutality; the uncompromising effect created.  In this photograph I was interested in using the preposterously excessive lower edge vignette to mirror the shapes and weights of the background hills.  Making the truck (bakkie) become a part of  or an echo of  the landscape.  I did a horse like that once too, though with a less extravagant vignette.<br/> <br/><a href="//www.dpchallenge.com/image.php?IMAGE_ID=918031" rel="nofollow" target="_blank"><img alt="'' . substr(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_challenge/1000-1999/1296/120/Copyrighted_Image_Reuse_Prohibited_918031.jpg'', strrpos(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_challenge/1000-1999/1296/120/Copyrighted_Image_Reuse_Prohibited_918031.jpg'', ''/'') + 1) . ''" border="0" src="//images.dpchallenge.com/images_challenge/1000-1999/1296/120/Copyrighted_Image_Reuse_Prohibited_918031.jpg"/></a></td>', 'Originally posted by bmartuch: This is very cool. I think it would be better without the vignetting. Originally posted by posthumous: vignette seems too heavy to me but I love the composition.  Originally posted by insteps: I don''t necessarily dislike the heavy vignette but I''d be interested in seeing the natural image.  I don''t know why I like severe vignettes so much; they''re just an affectation when done to this extent, and I know it.But I actually like the brutality; the uncompromising effect created.  In this photograph I was interested in using the preposterously excessive lower edge vignette to mirror the shapes and weights of the background hills.  Making the truck (bakkie) become a part of  or an echo of  the landscape.  I did a horse like that once too, though with a less extravagant vignette. ', '2014-11-03 13:04:27.000000', NULL, 0),
